   "Small mini-batches make detector training notoriously lengthy (ResNet-152 on COCO takes 3 days with batch 16 on 8 GPUs).",
   "Small mini-batches cannot re-train batch normalization, so detectors keep fixed ImageNet BN statistics that mismatch COCO.",
   "Positive/negative sample ratios are more imbalanced within small mini-batches, which may hurt final accuracy.",
   "The large-batch dilemma: the equivalent learning rate rule demands a large learning rate, but large learning rates in detection often fail to converge, while small ones give inferior results.",
   "Classification and detection tasks have different degrees of sensitivity to translation, so fine-tuning from classification networks creates a learning objective bias.",
   "The detector architecture is limited by the classification network; any architectural change requires costly re-pretraining on ImageNet.",
   "Detectors trained from scratch generally perform worse than pretrained ones and can suffer convergence issues, with unstable gradients falling into bad local minima.",
   "DSOD, the prior from-scratch detector, is bound to the DenseNet architecture and degrades or crashes when other network types are used.",
   "Most modern object detectors follow the pre-training and fine-tuning paradigm, and the belief that training from scratch is non-trivial became conventional wisdom.",
   "Improvements on object detection from large-scale pre-training are relatively small (about +1.5 AP on COCO with 3000x larger pre-training data) and the marginal benefit diminishes rapidly.",
   "Controlled comparisons using short training schedules can veil the true behavior of training from random initialization because the cost of ImageNet pre-training is often ignored.",
   "Prior from-scratch detectors (DSOD, DetNet, CornerNet) rely on detection-specific architectures, leaving the role of pre-training on unspecialized architectures untested.",
